@inject AbioticEditor.Web.Services.HostLanguageService Languages
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="@Languages.CurrentCode">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* Content-stamped addresses from the build manifest. The per-screen stylesheet bundle is
       regenerated in place by every build, and asking for it by plain name meant a window open
       during a build lost every one of those styles until it was reloaded by hand. *@
    @* parity.css, the fonts and the images now live in AbioticEditor.Web.Shared so the browser
       host serves the identical look from the identical files. A Razor Class Library's static
       assets are published under _content/<library>/, which is why these addresses changed. The
       scoped-CSS bundle stays named after THIS app: the SDK folds every referenced library's
       per-component styles into the consuming app's one bundle. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["AbioticEditor.Web.styles.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/AbioticEditor.Web.Shared/parity.css"]" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="@Assets["_content/AbioticEditor.Web.Shared/images/abiotic-factor.png"]" />
    <title>Abiotic Editor</title>
    @* Round 118: a server-rendered circuit that dies (an unhandled render exception, the process
       restarting, the window losing its connection) used to leave the player with a window that
       looked exactly like it had before the crash - every button dead, nothing on screen saying
       why, because blazor.web.js's own reconnection handling only ever TOGGLES CSS CLASSES on an
       element with this exact id; it never creates the element or supplies any visible styling of
       its own. Without both defined here there was nothing for it to show at all - see the crash
       report this round fixed, where the app "can't click any button anymore" after a render
       error killed the circuit. The three states blazor.web.js sets
       (components-reconnect-show/-failed/-rejected) all mean the same thing from here: the
       running game side of this window is no longer talking to the editor side, and reloading is
       the one action that always recovers it (this is a local desktop window, not a public site -
       a full reload is cheap and safe, never a lost multi-user session). *@
    <style>
        #components-reconnect-modal { display: none; }
        #components-reconnect-modal.components-reconnect-show,
        #components-reconnect-modal.components-reconnect-failed,
        #components-reconnect-modal.components-reconnect-rejected {
            display: flex;
            position: fixed;
            inset: 0;
            z-index: 9999;
            align-items: center;
            justify-content: center;
            flex-direction: column;
            gap: 0.75rem;
            background: rgba(10, 12, 10, 0.88);
            color: #F0EAD6;
            font: 15px/1.4 system-ui, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            padding: 1.5rem;
        }
        #components-reconnect-modal strong { font-size: 1.05rem; }
        #components-reconnect-modal button {
            margin-top: 0.25rem;
            padding: 0.5rem 1.25rem;
            font: inherit;
            font-weight: 600;
            color: #101810;
            background: #F5C518;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
        #components-reconnect-modal button:hover { filter: brightness(1.08); }
    </style>
    <HeadOutlet />
</head>
<body>
    <Routes />
    @* Always present, always hidden until blazor.web.js's own reconnection handler adds one of
       the classes styled above - see the remarks up in <head>. Reload always starts a brand-new
       circuit against this same local window, which is the only recovery a dead one has. *@
    <div id="components-reconnect-modal" role="alertdialog" aria-live="assertive">
        <strong>Connection lost</strong>
        <span>The editor lost its connection to the running app. Your last saved changes are safe on disk.</span>
        <button type="button" onclick="location.reload()">Reload</button>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_content/AbioticEditor.Web.Shared/modal.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
</body>
</html>
